Author-Name: Wunsch, Guillaume
Author-Email: g.wunsch@uclouvain.be
Author-Workplace-Name: Université catholique de Louvain, LIDAM/ISBA, Belgium
Author-Name: Mouchart, Michel 
Author-Email: michel.mouchart@uclouvain.be
Author-Workplace-Name: Université catholique de Louvain, LIDAM/ISBA, Belgium
Author-Name: Russo, Federica
Title: La modélisation en sciences sociales : incertitudes et défis 
Abstract: Mon père est un modèle pour moi, les rapports de l’artiste et de son modèle sont ambigus, le modèle capitaliste s’est répandu partout après la chute des régimes communistes, E. coli est un organisme modèle en génétique, les modèles nucléaires se sont affinés au long du XXe siècle, les ingénieurs utilisent quotidiennement de puissants modèles mathématiques… Le mot modèle transcende les disciplines et occupe une place centrale dans les savoirs. Ce livre rassemble des textes émanant de toutes les classes de l’Académie; chaque auteur y explore la notion de modèle dans son propre domaine.
Note: In: Modèles : prévoir, comprendre, expliquer, interpréter, reproduire, trahir, Académie Royale de Belgique  : Bruxelles 2020, p. 165-183
Series: UCLouvain, LIDAM, ISBA
Number: 2020023
Creation-Date: 2020-09-01
Handle: RePEc:aiz:louvar:2020023

Template-Type: ReDIF-Paper 1.0
Author-Name: Segers, Johan
Author-Email: johan.segers@uclouvain.be
Author-Workplace-Name: Université catholique de Louvain, LIDAM/ISBA, Belgium
Title: One- versus multi-component regular variation and extremes of Markov trees
Abstract: A Markov tree is a random vector indexed by the nodes of a tree whose distribution is determined by the distributions of pairs of neighbouring variables and a list of conditional independence relations. Upon an assumption on the tails of the Markov kernels associated to these pairs, the conditional distribution of the self-normalized random vector when the variable at the root of the tree tends to infinity converges weakly to a random vector of coupled random walks called tail tree. If, in addition, the conditioning variable has a regularly varying tail, the Markov tree satisfies a form of one-component regular variation. Changing the location of the root, that is, changing the conditioning variable, yields a different tail tree. When the tails of the marginal distributions of the conditioning variables are balanced, these tail trees are connected by a formula that generalizes the time change formula for regularly varying stationary time series. The formula is most easily understood when the various one-component regular variation statements are tied up to a single multi-component statement. The theory of multi-component regular variation is worked out for general random vectors, not necessarily Markov trees, with an eye towards other models, graphical or otherwise. Keywords: Conditional independence; graphical model; H¨usler–Reiss distribution; max-linear model; Markov tree; multivariate Pareto distribution; Pickands dependence function; regular variation; root change formula; tail measure; tail tree; time change formula.
Keywords: Conditional independence, graphical model, Hüsler–Reiss distribution, max-linear model, Markov tree, multivariate Pareto distribution, Pickands dependence function, regular variation,
root change formula, tail measure, tail tree, time change formula 
Note: In: Advances in Applied Probability - Vol. 52, no.3, p. 855-878 (2020)
Series: UCLouvain, LIDAM, ISBA
Number: 2020024
Creation-Date: 2020-09-24
DOI: https://doi.org/10.1017/apr.2020.22
Handle: RePEc:aiz:louvar:2020024

